\ A quotation reaching `when` or `unless` arrives as a runtime value, so the compiled body cannot
\ see its effect. The arm was modeled as having the same effect as the sibling empty arm, which
\ stored a stack pointer below whatever the quotation actually left. A result was orphaned, the
\ binary died with `unknown runtime error`, and a container result leaked. The dispatched arm
\ settles as a row instead, so the caller reads the real depth.
use "sequences" ;

mk: ( n -- arr ) [ 1array { 1 2 } #append ] ;

\ The same shape inside a word body, which the abstract stack used to reject outright.
pick5: ( -- n ) [ t [ 5 ] when ] ;

t [ 5 ] when .
f [ 5 ] when
"--" print-line
t [ 5 mk ] when .
"--" print-line
pick5 .
"--" print-line
f [ 5 ] unless .
t [ 5 ] unless
